# maintain_suite

The 10-fixture v1 acceptance bench. Strict gates dispatch through a
single pipeline; new fixtures must declare their per-requirement spec.

## Must
- `vacuous_test` gate fires on `implement` + `tests_pass` only
- `min_added_lines` is a floor, not a ceiling
- New fixtures land with a `requirements:` block (SpecDD Lever 1 schema)
- Bench runs use `--work-dir ~/.luxe/bench-workspace` (variance leak fix)
- Sidecar regrade via `scripts/regrade_local.py` re-runs the citation linter against `synthesizer.md`
- Bench runs PRUNE their own branch backlog: `_prune_old_branches` keeps the
  newest `LUXE_BENCH_BRANCH_RETENTION` (25) `<prefix>/*` branches PER GOAL SLUG
  in both the workspace clone and the fixture-cache origin, and logs what it
  dropped. Grouping is per slug because `luxe.pr.plan_branch_name`'s 99-name
  budget is per slug — a repo-wide cap would still let one hot slug die.
  Retention, not delete-after-grading: `regrade_local.py` checks the pushed
  branch out, so recent ones must survive. `keep<=0` is a no-op, never a wipe
- Borderline document/manage fixtures replicate 3× before claiming a regression
- A git failure during GRADING is a harness ERROR, never a verdict
  (2026-08-12): grade.py's diff/grep helpers raise `GitRunError` instead of
  returning the empty-diff shape ([] / (0,0) / ""), and run.py's per-fixture
  handler records the fixture as ERRORED — the scoreboard already separates
  that from FAILED. Before this, an index.lock race or corrupt base_sha
  graded every diff-dependent requirement as "the model changed nothing".
  `git grep` keeps rc 1 as a legitimate no-match (ok_codes=(0,1)); the
  vacuous-test worktree gate keeps its documented fail-open (it only ever
  removes points)

## Must not
- Run bench without `--keep-loaded` (post-run unload fires by default)
- Let the branch backlog grow unpruned. Before 2026-08-11 nothing removed bench
  branches, so a fixture died permanently at ~99 runs on one goal with
  `PRError: Cannot find a free branch name`. Because A/B arms run sequentially
  the arm running SECOND absorbed the exhaustion and its fixtures scored 0,
  which the scoreboard renders as an ordinary failure — it manufactured a
  clean-looking 116-vs-108 "regression" that was pure arm ordering. See
  `acceptance/pwir_rerun_2026_08_10/BRANCH-LEAK.md`
- Trust `scripts/regrade_local.py` results that read `origin/<branch>` (pre-v1.4.1 bug; fixed but stay alert)
- Add fixtures without `task_type` in the YAML (cve_lookup gating depends on it)

## Owns
- benchmarks/maintain_suite/**
